    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d607">

                <pb facs="0808" n="718" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg718"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">814.00/328: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Scotten</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-01-03T16:00:00-06:36"><hi rendition="#italic">January 3,
                                1920—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 5—2:38
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1. My December 31, 8 p.m.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d607-f1"/></p>
                <p>Pamphlets distributed January 1 causing wide spread comment. Legation reliably
                    informed by several sources, all of which agree, nomenclature new party purely
                    cloak to cover true aim, namely downfall President Cabrera. Signers of pamphlet
                    for the most part men of means and standing and many are known to be clericals
                    and sympathizers with Bishop Piñol. All of Legation’s informants<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d607-f2"/> believe that these men will not be easily intimidated and
                    that distribution of pamphlets marks beginning of the end of present regime.
                    Impression general that new party has at least approval, if not support of
                    Government of the United States. One of Legation’s most reliable informants
                    states that result of Legation’s negotiations with President as regards sale of
                    enemy’s properties will have considerable effect upon standing and strength of
                    new party.</p>
                <p>Legation informed by indubitable source that Antonio C. Rivera, director of
                    military academy at the time of attempted assassination of the President 1908,
                    arrested and held in jail several hours last night on the charge of being the
                    leader of new party but finally released.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Scotten</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
